## Why This Is Asked

This question tests understanding of three-valued logic in a practical policy engine context, requiring the design of a backtracking propagator that navigates partial knowledge while avoiding false constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic (T > U > F)
- Implication as ¬A ∨ B under three-valued semantics
- Constraint propagation with Unknown values
- Backtracking search with pruning to avoid False outcomes

## Code Example

```javascript
// Kleene three-valued logic helpers
const T = 'T', F = 'F', U = 'U';
function not(x){ return x==='T'?'F': x==='F'?'T':'U'; }
function and(x,y){ return x===F||y===F?F : x===T&&y===T?T : U; }
function or(x,y){ return x===T||y===T?T : x===F&&y===F?F : U; }
function implies(x,y){ return or(not(x),y); }

// Backtracking propagation
function solve(){
  const vars = {F1:U,F2:U,F3:U,F4:U,F5:U};
  const constraints = [
    () => implies(vars.F1, vars.F2),
    () => implies(vars.F2, vars.F3),
    () => implies(or(vars.F3, vars.F4), vars.F5),
    () => implies(not(vars.F5), or(vars.F1, vars.F2))
  ];

  function backtrack(idx){
    if(idx === 5) return constraints.every(c => c() !== F);
    for(const val of [T,U,F]){
      vars['F'+(idx+1)] = val;
      if(constraints.every(c => c() !== F) && backtrack(idx+1)) return true;
    }
    return false;
  }

  return backtrack(0) ? vars : null;
}
```

## Sample Run

The algorithm explores assignments systematically, pruning branches where any constraint evaluates to False. It finds the solution F1=U, F2=T, F3=U, F4=U, F5=T, where all constraints evaluate to either True or Unknown, satisfying the requirement to avoid False outcomes.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason with non-classical truth in a constrained system, using backtracking under Kleene semantics and additional operators (↔, ⊕) to reveal subtle propagation patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued semantics and derived operators
- Backtracking with constraint propagation
- Handling composite operators like biconditional and XOR under 3-valued logic

## Code Example
```javascript
function not(v){return v==='T'?'F':v==='F'?'T':'U';}
function imp(a,b){ // a→b = (¬a) ∨ b
  const na = not(a);
  return (na==='T' || b==='T') ? 'T' : (na==='U' || b==='U') ? 'U' : 'F';
}
function orb(a,b){ // max in T>U>F
  const order = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
  return order[a] >= order[b] ? a : b;
}
function land(a,b){ // min in T>U>F
  const order = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
  return order[a] <= order[b] ? a : b;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
To gauge practical reasoning about logic constraints, Horn-SAT familiarity, and ability to reason about linear-time algorithms and incremental updates in data pipelines.

## Key Concepts
- Horn clauses
- Forward chaining
- Partial vs full assignments
- Complexity O(n+m)
- Cycle handling and inconsistency detection

## Code Example
```javascript
function solveHorn(rules, facts){
  const trueSet = new Set(facts);
  let queue = rules.filter(r => r.antecedent.every(v => trueSet.has(v)));
  while (queue.length){
    const r = queue.shift();
    if (!trueSet.has(r.consequent)){
      trueSet.add(r.consequent);
      queue = rules.filter(x => x.antecedent.every(v => trueSet.has(v)) && !trueSet.has(x.consequent));
    }
  }
  return trueSet;
}
```

